Introduction
This series of interactive articles tries to take commonly used genomic and transcriptomic bioinformatic tools and explain their underlying mechanisms. In this article, we will explore the seemingly magical algorithm of Burrows-Wheeler Transform (BWT), also known as block-sorting compression. BWT is not particularly intuitive to the human processing, especially with repetitive genomic sequences but can reach O(n) time with optimal algorithms.
Burrows-Wheeler transform has two main purposes and you have likely encountered them: 1. Pre-processing step to data compression: The BWT serves as the foundation for a number of lossless data compression algorithms (you may have heard of bzip or bzip2), some of which are employed for maintaining massive amounts of genetic sequencing data. 2. Short-read mapping: Several modern short-read aligners, including bowtie, bwa, and soap2, efficiently build an index of a given reference genome using algorithms based on the BWT.
Below are some helper functions used for the calculations in this tutorial.


Code
function cleanInput(s) {
  s = s.replaceAll(" ", "")
  s = s.replaceAll("$", "")
  s = s + "$"
  return s
}

function getRotations(s) {
  const n = s.length;
  return Array.from({length: n}, (_, k) =&gt;
    Array.from({length: n}, (_, i) =&gt; s[(i + k) % n]).join("")
  );
}

function getSortedRotations(s) {
  return getRotations(s).sort();
}

function computeBWT(s) {
  return getSortedRotations(s)
    .map(r =&gt; r[r.length - 1])
    .join("");
}

function buildTable(rotations, n) {
  const tbody = document.createElement("tbody")

  rotations.forEach((rotation) =&gt; {   // ← "sorted" becomes "rotations"
    const tr = document.createElement("tr")

    rotation.split("").forEach((char, colIndex) =&gt; {
      const td = document.createElement("td")
      td.textContent = char

      td.style.cssText = `
        padding: 6px 18px;
        font-family: monospace;
        font-size: 1.1em;
        text-align: center;
      `

      // $ highlighting
      if (char === "$") {
        td.style.color = "#0369a1"
        td.style.fontWeight = "bold"
      }

      // First column → blue
      if (colIndex === 0) {
        td.style.backgroundColor = "#e0f2fe"
        td.style.fontWeight = "bold"
        td.style.color = "#0369a1"
      }

      // Last column → indigo
      if (colIndex === n - 1) {
        td.style.backgroundColor = "#4f46e5"
        td.style.color = "white"
        td.style.fontWeight = "bold"
      }

      tr.appendChild(td)
    })

    tbody.appendChild(tr)
  })

  const table = document.createElement("table")
  table.style.cssText = `
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  `
  table.appendChild(tbody)

  return table
}

function inverseBWTSteps(bwt) {
  const n = bwt.length
  let matrix = bwt.split("").sort()

  const steps = [matrix]  // step 0: just sorted single chars

  for (let i = 0; i &lt; n - 1; i++) {
    matrix = matrix.map((row, j) =&gt; bwt[j] + row)
    matrix = matrix.sort()
    steps.push(matrix)    // ✅ save each step
  }

  return steps
}

The Rotation Matrix
First, let’s look at the data compression step. In essence, the BWT manipulates a block of input text using a reversible transformation, this in itself, does not compress the text but reorders it (permutes) in a way that facilitates compression. Let’s first take a look at the rotation matrix below, currently showing the classic example “banana”, but feel free to change this to investigate how different strings are organized.
To find the BWT, we can first build the Burrows-Wheeler Matrix (BWM) which is a sorted list of all possible rotations of the given string.


Code
viewof userInput = Inputs.text({
  label: "Your string:",
  placeholder: "try banana",
  value: "banana"
})

cleaned = cleanInput(userInput)
rotations = getRotations(cleaned)
sortedRotations = getSortedRotations(cleaned)
bwt = computeBWT(cleaned)
n = cleaned.length

// Creates a flexbox view of an unsorted matrix, sorted matrix, and resulting BWT all from the given string.
{
  const unsorted = rotations
  const sorted = sortedRotations

  if (!cleaned || cleaned.length &lt;= 1) 
    return html`&lt;p&gt;Please enter a string above.&lt;/p&gt;`

  const table1 = buildTable(unsorted, n)
  const table2 = buildTable(sorted, n)

  return html`
    &lt;div style="display: flex; gap: 2rem; flex-wrap: wrap;"&gt;
      &lt;div&gt;
        &lt;h4&gt;① Write down all rotations&lt;/h4&gt;
        ${table1}
      &lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;div&gt;
        &lt;h4&gt;② Sort strings (row-wise)&lt;/h4&gt;
        ${table2}
      &lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;div&gt;
        &lt;h4&gt;③ BWT is the last column&lt;/h4&gt;
        &lt;p style="font-family: monospace; font-size: 1.2em;"&gt;${bwt}&lt;/p&gt;
      &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  `
}




















































What is the $ Symbol
The $ symbol is indicating the end of the string and is necessary to make the BWT fully reversible. Without the $ marker, you can still regenerate the matrix, but you wouldn’t be able to tell which rotation was the original format. All of these issues become amplified when looking at large genomic data.

Reversibility
Of course, any sorting algorithm can make a string easily more compressible. e.g. If we just lexographically sort banana -&gt; aaabnn -&gt; character frequency list. But the beauty of BWT is that the compressed string can be reversed into the original string.
The logic in reversing BWT using the BWM follows that the BWT is the sorted version of each previous one (fix whatever this explanation is). Therefore, given the encoded string, we can reconstruct the matrix by starting wtih an empty matrix, then with each step, prepending the BWT string, sorting the strings, and repeating until the matrix is filled. Move the slider to see each step.


Code
viewof step = Inputs.range([0, n-1], {  // ← max value?
  step: 1,
  label: "Step",
  value: n-1
})

allSteps = inverseBWTSteps(bwt)

{
  const currentMatrix = allSteps[step]
  const cols = currentMatrix[0].length  // how wide is matrix at this step?

  return html`
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;p&gt;Step ${step} of ${n-1}&lt;/p&gt;
      ${buildTable(currentMatrix, cols)}
      ${step === n-1 ? html`&lt;p&gt;✅ Original string: &lt;strong&gt;${currentMatrix.find(row =&gt; row.slice(-1) === "$")}&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;` : ""}
    &lt;/div&gt;
  `
}

Short-read Mapping
Now you have a good grasp on what BWT is, how to generate and ungenerate it. But how does this help with short-read mapping? Well here’s where we get into the fun properties that come BWT and perform Last-to-First Mapping (LF Mapping).
LF mapping can help us find a substring within the larger string using the property that the first column is just the last column in sorted order. And with this property, the character in the first column is just the character that comes after the character in the last column. F is the character that immediately follows L. L in the original string S becuase each row is a cyclic rotation.


Code
// Interactive Element 5: LF Mapping Visualization
{
  if (!cleaned || cleaned.length &lt;= 1)
    return html`&lt;p&gt;Please enter a string above.&lt;/p&gt;`

  const sorted = getSortedRotations(cleaned)
  const n = cleaned.length

  const F = sorted.map(r =&gt; r[0])
  const L = sorted.map(r =&gt; r[r.length - 1])

  // ── ADD THEAD HERE ──
  const thead = document.createElement("thead")
  const headerRow = document.createElement("tr")
  ["F", "L"].forEach(label =&gt; {
    const th = document.createElement("th")
    th.textContent = label
    th.style.cssText = `
      padding: 4px 20px;
      font-family: monospace;
      color: #4f46e5;
      border-bottom: 2px solid #e5e7eb;
    `
    headerRow.appendChild(th)
  })
  thead.appendChild(headerRow)

  const tbody = document.createElement("tbody")

  F.forEach((fChar, rowIndex) =&gt; {
    const tr = document.createElement("tr")

    const tdF = document.createElement("td")
    tdF.textContent = fChar
    // ── ADD STYLING HERE ──
    tdF.style.cssText = `
      padding: 8px 20px;
      font-family: monospace;
      font-size: 1.2em;
      text-align: center;
      cursor: pointer;
      border-bottom: 1px solid #f3f4f6;
    `

    const tdL = document.createElement("td")
    tdL.textContent = L[rowIndex]
    // ── ADD STYLING HERE ──
    tdL.style.cssText = `
      padding: 8px 20px;
      font-family: monospace;
      font-size: 1.2em;
      text-align: center;
      cursor: pointer;
      border-bottom: 1px solid #f3f4f6;
    `

    tdF.addEventListener("mouseover", () =&gt; {
      tr.style.backgroundColor = "#e0f2fe"
    })

    tdF.addEventListener("mouseout", () =&gt; {
      tr.style.backgroundColor = ""
    })

    tr.appendChild(tdF)
    tr.appendChild(tdL)
    tbody.appendChild(tr)
  })

  const table = document.createElement("table")
  table.style.cssText = `
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin-top: 1rem;
  `
  // ── ADD THEAD TO TABLE HERE ──
  table.appendChild(thead)
  table.appendChild(tbody)

  return table
}







Say we want to find the substring bana in banana, we’d start from the last character and go backwards.
ADD INTERACTIVE ELEMENT 6: SUBSTRING FINDING ANIMATION.
Wait, if this can be used for finding a substring, what if our substring is just the whole string? Exactly right. This LF mapping can also be used to reconstruct our original string in a way that is more efficient.
ADD INTERACTIVE ELEMENT 7: RECONSTRUCTING ORIGINAL STRING USING LF MAPPING

Efficient sorting
We have everything we need now. Our computer can just compute the BWT and store it right? Well yes, but then when we reconstruct it, we’d need to scan for the corresponding letter each time, and this is very computationally expensive. How can we mitigate this? With a simple count matrix, we can simply select the next element instead of scanning each time, reducing from X to X time.
